From: y52s recovery work
Subject: [PATCH] y52s: open the SID enrolment with the synthetic password

The previous patch assumed user 0 held two gatekeeper enrolments protected by
two different passwords.  AOSP's source says otherwise, and the file times on
the device confirm it: both files were written in the same
setLockCredential() pass (2024-01-19 16:42:28), so neither is a leftover.

What the two handles really are:

  spblob/<sp>.pwd -> PasswordData.passwordHandle
      createPasswordBasedSyntheticPassword():
          enroll(fakeUid(user), null, null, passwordTokenToGkInput(pwdToken))
      uid domain 100000+user, secret = scrypt(PIN) -> so the PIN opens it.
      Measured SID 0xc8dbe6498e82c2bb.

  spblob/<16 hex>.handle -> the SID handle
      newSidForUser():
          enroll(user, null, null, authToken.deriveGkPassword())
      uid domain = the REAL user id, secret = KDF(syntheticPassword,
      "sp-gk-authentication").  Measured SID 0x0779b8423e32ee87.

The app-clone escrow key (1000_USRPKEY_profile_key_name_decrypt_999) is bound
to USER_SECURE_ID 0x0779b8423e32ee87, i.e. the second one, which is why every
token minted from the PIN returned -26: right device, wrong secret, wrong uid
domain.  No byte-order permutation can fix that, and each rejected verify only
adds to gatekeeper's throttle.

So: verify the PasswordData handle alone (the PIN can only open that one), and
mint the SID token from the synthetic password exactly like AOSP does -
SP800-108 for SP v3, personalisedHash for v2, both under the AOSP label
"sp-gk-authentication" and the AOSP context.  PersonalizedHashSP800() is
already the call this file uses to produce the disk decryption key, so the
label is the only new input.  The SP is cached as it is unwrapped, so a
profile unlocked in the same run is one HIDL round trip away.

Also stop hardcoding /data/misc/keystore/user_0/1000_USRPKEY_...: enumerate
the parent's keystore directory for the suffix the profile needs.

Verified offline: chain13 + 0011 + 0012 + 0013 replays byte-identically.

